    {
      "id": "o8p1LH90FA07OsazVtcYV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1544.8705642108764,
      "y": 70.40131014674876,
      "width": 745.1612577027756,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#da77f2",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBZ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1041999971,
      "version": 706,
      "versionNonce": 1591566467,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1766593611216,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Frequent driver location Update & proximity search on location data:\nassuming 10M drivers sending location every 5 sec is around 2M wps. which\nis huge.\n- Bad Solution: direct db writes + proximity queries\n- Good Solution: batch processing + geospatial databse for geospatial\nindex(Quad Trees).\n- Using redis inmemory data store to handle real time location update\nrequests. It provides geohashing for proximity search as well.\n- we can further optimize this by using adaptive location update intervals.\ni.e dynamically adjusting the freq of location updates based on contextual\nfactors like spee, direction, distance etc.\n\n2) Preventing multiple ride requests to same driver:\n- good solution: request sent to driver and driver status marked as\noutstanding_request. If the driver accepts the request, we update the\nstatus to \"accepted\" and if they deny it, we update the status to\n\"available\". We can then use a simple timeout mechanism within the Ride\nService to ensure that the lock is released if the driver does not respond\nwithin the 10 second window.\n- great Solution: Using distributed lock with ttl When a ride request is sent\nto a driver, a lock is created with a unique identifier (e.g., driverId) and a\nTTL set to the acceptance window duration of 10 seconds. The Ride\nMatching Service attempts to acquire a lock on the driverId in Redis. If\nthe lock is successfully acquired, it means no other service instance can\nsend a ride request to the same driver until the lock expires or is released.\nIf the driver accepts the ride within the TTL window, the Ride Matching\nService updates the ride status to \"accepted\" in the database, and the\nlock is released in Redis. If the driver does not accept the ride within the\nTTL window, the lock in Redis expires automatically. This expiration allows\nthe Ride Matching Service to consider the driver for new ride requests\nagain.\n\n3) Ensuring no ride requests are dropped during peak demand periods; we\nintroduce a queue with dynamic scaling.\n\nfurther scaling can be done with geosharding and read replicas.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Frequent driver location Update & proximity search on location data: assuming 10M drivers sending location every 5 sec is around 2M wps. which is huge.\n- Bad Solution: direct db writes + proximity queries\n- Good Solution: batch processing + geospatial databse for geospatial index(Quad Trees).\n- Using redis inmemory data store to handle real time location update requests. It provides geohashing for proximity search as well.\n- we can further optimize this by using adaptive location update intervals. i.e dynamically adjusting the freq of location updates based on contextual factors like spee, direction, distance etc.\n\n2) Preventing multiple ride requests to same driver:\n- good solution: request sent to driver and driver status marked as outstanding_request. If the driver accepts the request, we update the status to \"accepted\" and if they deny it, we update the status to \"available\". We can then use a simple timeout mechanism within the Ride Service to ensure that the lock is released if the driver does not respond within the 10 second window.\n- great Solution: Using distributed lock with ttl When a ride request is sent to a driver, a lock is created with a unique identifier (e.g., driverId) and a TTL set to the acceptance window duration of 10 seconds. The Ride Matching Service attempts to acquire a lock on the driverId in Redis. If the lock is successfully acquired, it means no other service instance can send a ride request to the same driver until the lock expires or is released. If the driver accepts the ride within the TTL window, the Ride Matching Service updates the ride status to \"accepted\" in the database, and the lock is released in Redis. If the driver does not accept the ride within the TTL window, the lock in Redis expires automatically. This expiration allows the Ride Matching Service to consider the driver for new ride requests again.\n\n3) Ensuring no ride requests are dropped during peak demand periods; we introduce a queue with dynamic scaling.\n\nfurther scaling can be done with geosharding and read replicas.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
